COUNTY COUNSEL IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS- SAN BERNARDINO COMMUNITY COLLEGE
SUBJECT DISTRICT BOND MEASURE ELECTION TO BE HELD ON NOVEMBER 3, 2026
This Measure was placed on the ballot by a resolution adopted by the Board of Trustees of the San
Bernardino Community College District (District). A "yes" vote by at least fifty-five percent of the voters
voting on this Measure will authorize the District to issue bonds in the amount of $898,000,000 to be used
solely for the purposes set forth in this ballot Measure. A "no" vote means that the District will not be
authorized to issue and sell the $898,000,000 in bonds set forth in the Measure.
The District's Resolution was adopted pursuant to California Education Code sections 15100 et seq. and
15264, et seq., Government Code section 53506, and Articles XIIIA and XVI of the California Constitution.
The bond proceeds may only be used for specific projects at San Bernardino Valley College and Crafton
Hills College listed in Exhibits "A" and "B" of the Measure (Projects). The identified Projects are the specific
projects the District proposes to finance with proceeds from the sale of the bonds authorized by this ballot
Measure and the bond proceeds may only be used for those specific projects. These projects include:
affordable career education facilities for firefighters and skilled trades; local job training programs to prepare
students for good-paying careers in nursing, teaching and firefighting; removing asbestos and mold;
repairing deteriorating gas lines; upgrading emergency communications systems; upgrading campus safety
and security, including fire safety systems; providing safe drinking water; repairing and upgrading
classrooms and labs; repairing and upgrading air conditioning and heating systems; earthquake retrofitting
school buildings and classrooms; creating a community college center in the mountain area of the District;
upgrading classrooms and labs and career training facilities for nursing, teaching, and firefighting; improving
student centers to provide job training, job placement, counseling, and support services to students; and
other repair, renovation and construction projects.
The District will conduct financial and performance audits annually and all bond expenditures will be
monitored by an independent Citizens' Oversight Committee, in compliance with Education Code section
15278 et seq., that will receive and review annual audits, inspect District facilities, receive copies of
maintenance plans, and review efforts to implement cost-saving programs. No District employees or
vendors will be allowed to serve on the Citizens' Oversight Committee.
The bonded debt will be a general obligation of the District and will be financed by taxes levied annually on
taxable property located within the District.
